# Retina-aware Match Client

Approved scope: macOS Client to macOS Host matches current logical desktop
dimensions and backing pixels. A 1710x1107-point desktop at 2x requests
3420x2214 encoded pixels, not a 3420x2214-point desktop or blurry 1710x1107
video. Read the current display mode, not its panel-native maximum: scaled
"Looks like" settings can have different compositor and panel resolutions.

Display preparation schema 3 carries explicit integer scale 1 or 2. The Host
registers logical virtual modes with hiDPI enabled for 2x and confirms both
CGDisplayMode backing pixels and logical bounds. Reconnect/recovery retain the
same scale. The existing topology already represents both coordinate systems;
input mapping and capture continue to use it. Launch schema remains 2.

Manual modes and Linux Client Match Client remain 1x. Linux Host, EDID, codec,
transport and permission policies are unchanged. One or two horizontal client
displays must share a scale; mixed-scale layouts fail with manual-mode guidance
rather than approximating one display. Matching candidate Host/Client versions
are required; no old-schema fallback or silent geometry substitution.

The virtual-display mode convention was cross-checked against Chromium's
[macOS virtual-display test utility](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/HEAD/ui/display/mac/test/virtual_display_util_mac.mm).
It uses logical mode dimensions with HiDPI and a backing-pixel descriptor.
This is a reference, not proof of live behavior on the target OS.

Gates: shared Client topology/request-vector tests, synthetic Host 1x/2x mode
selection and recovery, authenticated loopback display preparation, malformed
schema/scale and permission-denial cases, signed hosted Host/Client builds.
Live acceptance still requires the laptop's Default/Scaled settings, readable
UI with sharp text, pointer/click alignment, reconnect, and manual-mode 1x
restoration. Package compilation alone cannot qualify the undocumented virtual
display API. No production session interruption without operator coordination.
